# Tests

The test layers are in `docs/design.ja.md`. This rule is how a test is written.

## Framework

- xUnit v3 with its built-in `Assert`. No assertion library, no mocking library: hand-written fakes behind the interfaces the design names.
- Tests run on Microsoft.Testing.Platform (`global.json` `test.runner`). There is no `Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk` or `xunit.runner.visualstudio`. Run one project with `dotnet test --project <path>`, and some of its tests with `dotnet test --project <path> -- --filter-method <pattern>`. Read the exit code and the count of tests run: a run that ran no test is not a pass.
- Time comes from a fake `TimeProvider` (`Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing`). A test never sleeps, never waits on the wall clock, and never asserts what the clock decides.
- Keys are generated in the test or in a fixture shared by one test class, at run time. A test never reads a key or a token from the repository.
- A server test runs the server in process on a loopback port the OS picks, and stops it in the test's cleanup.
- Every run of `./build.ps1` writes its output to a file in the scratchpad, so that a failure seen once can be named.

## Structure

- `tests/<Project>.Tests` mirrors `src/<Project>`. `Foo.cs` → `FooTests.cs`, in the same folder.
- Method names: `{Target}_{Scenario}_{Expected}` in PascalCase, English.
- Each test owns its state. Tests run in parallel; a test that cannot says why in its collection definition.

## Documentation

Every `[Fact]` and `[Theory]` has XML docs in this shape, in English:

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// One line: the behavior this test guards.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// Preconditions:
/// - ...
///
/// Steps:
/// - ...
///
/// Expected:
/// - ...
/// </remarks>
[Fact]
public void Verify_PayloadFieldChanged_ReturnsInvalidSignature()
{
}
```

- A `[Theory]` is documented once; list what the cases cover under Preconditions or Steps.
- No custom XML tags. Link a design section when the why is not obvious.

## What a test checks

- A test stands for the whole design statement (`.cursor/rules/spec-checks.mdc`).
- Compare with an independent reference: an RFC test vector, a token produced by a reviewed earlier run, a value worked out by hand. Do not compare a function with a re-implementation of itself.
- A memo or cache key of several values is tested by changing each value alone, the others kept.
- A test of a comparison (a priority, a tie-break, the oldest lease) sets up candidates whose values differ.
- Mutation check for a guard: change the guarded term and see the test fail, then restore it by copying the file aside and back (not `git checkout`). List the mutations in the PR. Every test of a security check gets this before the PR.
- A test of one check uses an input that passes every check before it and breaks only that one. In M3a the test that signed a lease with another key also changed its `kid`, so the `kid` check rejected it first, and the mutant that removed the signature check survived; the test of the proof's `kid` against its key had the same shape (PR #16).
- A check that a later layer also refuses needs a test that observes what only that check does. In M3b the read limit of a request body was hidden by the `Content-Length` check before it, and the first-connection TLS check by the check on every connection after it; mutants of both survived until the tests sent a chunked body and asserted that no pin was saved (PR #22).
- A limit is tested exactly at it and one past it, and a `>` → `>=` mutant shows that both sides are pinned. In M3b the tests sent only the values over the limits (a version of 33 characters, 9 fingerprints, a host of 256 bytes), and the review found that nothing checked the accepted side (PR #22). A limit counted in UTF-8 bytes gets a multi-byte character in the value at the limit. In M3c the review found the accepted side missing again, for seven new limits (PR #27): before the PR, list each limit the change adds with its two tests.
- A test that creates a machine-scope folder (an installation key, a server data folder) creates it under a folder of the test, not directly in the system's temporary folder. The machine-scope folder check refuses a parent that other users can write, and `/tmp` on Linux is such a parent; in M3c a test that passed on Windows failed on the Linux runner (PR #27).
- A mutant must build. With warnings as errors, a mutant such as `if (false)` fails to compile (CS0162), `dotnet test --no-build` then runs the previous binaries, and the mutant looks survived. Check the build's exit code before reading the test result, and write the mutant so that it compiles. The style and nullable analyzers fail a build too: in M2 `if ( (uses & ...` failed SA1008, and passing a variable the compiler sees as maybe null failed CS8604 (PR #12). Write a mutant as code that would pass review: `if (uses < KeyUses.None || ...)` instead of deleting the first operand. Deleting a whole `if` block fails too, when it leaves a parameter unused (IDE0060) or two blank lines in a row (SA1507); in M3b five such mutants did not build (PR #22). Add a condition that cannot hold instead: `if (record.Stopped && record.Count < 0)`.
- Each error case asserts its own message, not only the exit code or the exception type. In M2 the error cases ran as a batch after the test had changed the passphrase, and the case for a server option on a standard license exited with 2 because of the passphrase, not the option; a mutant found it (PR #12).
- Before running a mutant, name an input for which it returns something else than the original. A mutant that cannot differ looks survived and proves nothing: in M1, `if (!true || !guard.Observe(...))` is the original condition, and `Equals(a, a) && !x` is `!x` (PR #8).
- A script that runs mutants decides from the exit code of `dotnet test`, not from its text: Microsoft.Testing.Platform exits with 2 when a test failed, and its messages follow the OS language.
- Read the state you assert before a shared helper's cleanup runs. An assertion after a helper that resets state checks the helper, not the code.
